{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB12141 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Gilteritinib, a multitargeted tyrosine kinase inhibitor, is shown to have a potential relationship with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) with the translocation T(9;11)(p22;q23). The evidence suggests that Gilteritinib may be repurposed for this subset of AMLs, as it is indicated for Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia with or without maturation, and both of these subtypes are associated with the same target, Idarubicin. The shared indication for Idarubicin with AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23 indicates a possible mechanistic link between Gilteritinib and this disease. The evidence is supported by the presence of a common pathway involving Idarubicin, which is a known chemotherapeutic agent that targets leukemia cells, and the potential for Gilteritinib to modulate similar pathways. The integration of these pathways suggests that Gilteritinib may exert its effects through mechanisms that are relevant to AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23, although the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
